
Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Speech
$synth = New-Object System.Speech.Synthesis.SpeechSynthesizer
$synth.Rate = -1
$synth.Volume = 100
$synth.Speak("Here is where we are, Ignacio.  You have a working astronomy software project on your computer, in the folder C colon backslash Users backslash astro backslash fita.  It has two parts.  The first part is called FITA. That is the file format. Think of it as a layered Photoshop file for astronomical images, but built on top of the standard FITS format that all telescopes use. Each layer holds one band of light, from ultraviolet to infrared, with its own calibration, blend mode, and transparency mask. You can save and load these files right now.  The second part is called URANODYNE. That is the science engine built on top of FITA. It downloads sky images from NASA SkyView, stacks them into a FITA cube, detects stars that have moved between old and new sky surveys, and exports publication-quality images and spectral energy distribution plots.  You already ran the full pipeline on the Dumbbell Nebula, Messier 27. The output file is called m27 full dot fita. It contains twelve layers spanning from ultraviolet to mid-infrared. It is three hundred and ninety six megabytes on your disk.  What can you do right now?   Type:  python main.py  That opens a menu. Option one runs a live demo that queries SIMBAD, VizieR, and opens an interactive Aladin sky viewer in your browser. Option two reruns the full pipeline. Option three verifies all your existing outputs. Option six shows you the layer structure of the cube you already have.  The three things we just finished today are: one, the code is now cleanly split into fita and uranodyne as two separate installable packages. Two, the proper motion detection now masks the nebula before searching, and can cross-check against Gaia catalog stars. Three, you can now send any fita file to SAO DS9 using the SAMP protocol, which works natively on Windows without any compilation.  You are not lost. You have a real, working, tested codebase. Start with:  python main.py")
